// Coherence over method NAMES: two traits may not each hand one type a default body for the same
// method. A method table has one slot per name (there is no overloading), and nothing here says
// which body belongs in it — so it is E0027, like every other contest coherence settles, and like
// the bundle spelling of the identical collision ("binding `vec.SatKernels` would make the name
// ambiguous"). It used to be resolved by `HashMap` iteration order, which made this very file check
// green in one process and fail E0007 in the next.
// expect: error E0027 at 21:10
// expect: error E0007 at 29:14
// expect: exit 1
trait Greet {
    fn hello(): string { return "greet" }
}

trait Wave {
    fn hello(): int { return 42 }
}

struct P {
    x: int
    impl Greet {}
    impl Wave {}
}

// The binding is annotated deliberately: under the old hash-order winner this line was where the
// coin flip became visible — `hello` typed as `string` from `Greet` (E0007) or as `int` from `Wave`
// (green), about half the time each. It is also what makes this case load-bearing for the
// determinism gate's verdict digest, which compares the checker's diagnostics across processes.
// The E0007 below is that cascade, now stable: the ambiguity above is the fault to fix.
mut n: int = P { x: 1 }.hello()
echo n
